sections.s2_technical:
Let me contrast the two carefully, because choosing the wrong one leads to awkward schemas and surprising performance.

`@ElementCollection` is for data that is OWNED by one parent and has no independent existence. The elements are either basic types or `@Embeddable` value objects. They do not have an `@Id`. They live in a collection table whose foreign key points back to the parent, and they are loaded and saved entirely as part of the parent.

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String accountId;

    // collection of BASIC values
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_phones", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @Column(name = "phone")
    private List<String> phoneNumbers = new ArrayList<>();

    // collection of EMBEDDABLE value objects
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_nominees", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @OrderColumn(name = "position")          // avoids delete-all-reinsert on update
    private List<Nominee> nominees = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Embeddable
public class Nominee {
    private String name;
    private String relationship;
    private int sharePercent;
    // NO @Id — it is a value, not an entity
}
```

`@OneToMany` is for relationships to independent ENTITIES. Each child has its own `@Id`, its own table, and its own lifecycle. You can query the child directly, reference it from other entities, and manage it on its own.

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String accountId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();  // Trade is an ENTITY
}

@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id private String tradeId;                       // has its own identity
    @ManyToOne @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private Account account;
}
```

The decision rule I apply: ask "does this child have an identity, and would anyone ever load or query it on its own?" A trade has a trade id and auditors query trades directly, so it is an entity and I use `@OneToMany`. A nominee or a phone number is just descriptive data attached to one account; nobody queries "all phone numbers" as first-class records, so they are value objects and I use `@ElementCollection`.

Now the critical Hibernate caveat for `@ElementCollection`. If the collection table has no primary key and no `@OrderColumn`, Hibernate cannot identify individual rows, so on ANY modification it performs a DELETE of all rows for that parent followed by a full REINSERT. For a nominee list of three that is harmless; for a large value collection it is a performance trap. The fixes are to add an `@OrderColumn` (which gives each row a position and lets Hibernate target individual rows) or to ensure the embeddable has fields that together form an effective key. This is a frequent senior-level gotcha.

Two more points. First, `@ElementCollection` defaults to LAZY, like other collections, which is what you usually want. Second, because value objects have no identity, they cannot be shared between parents — each parent owns its own copies. If you find yourself wanting to share a child between two parents, that child is really an entity and belongs in a `@ManyToMany` or a pair of `@OneToMany`/`@ManyToOne` relationships, not an `@ElementCollection`.

